use std
into simprep.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sr_ is exported). It builds lightweight stand-ins for assembly components (simplified representations): the convex hull of a part's mesh points (an envelope that keeps the outer silhouette and drops all inner detail) and a tight box. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn sr_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sr_view_f64";
extern fn sr_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sr_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NV` (input points), `NC` (candidate points), `NT` (hull triangles), `CAP` (room for hull triangles). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (16 inputs), `OUT` (16 outputs), `MP` (9 per output triangle: its three corner positions), `MN` (9 per output triangle: the triangle's unit normal repeated for each corner), `ML` (6 per output line: its two end points), `VP` (3 per input point: x, y, z), `HV` (3 per candidate point), `BX` (15: box center x, y, z, then three unit axes of 3 each, then three half sizes), `FN` (4 per triangle slot: the unit outward normal x, y, z and the plane offset, the dot product of the normal and the triangle's first corner). Growable i32 arrays: `CI` (NC: the input point index of each candidate), `TR` (3 per triangle slot: corner candidate indices), `TA` (1 per triangle slot: 1 while the triangle is on the hull), `MK` (NV marks), `HE` (2 per horizon edge, room for 3 times CAP), `OT` (3 per output triangle, the final hull with corners renumbered), `OV` (NC: for each candidate, its output vertex number or -1).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again. `at1(n)` returns n, or 1 when n is below 1.

The exported function `sr_views()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, registers the arrays with `sr_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 VP, 4 HV, 5 BX, 6 MP, 7 MN, 8 ML; and with `sr_view_i32`: 20 OT. It returns 0.

The exported function `sr_init()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, sizes IN to 16 zeros, OUT to 16 zeros, BX to 15 zeros and MP, MN and ML to 1 zero each with `zf`, calls `sr_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `sr_size(nv)`, declared with the export keyword with one i32 parameter (converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NV to nv, sizes VP with `zf` to 3 nv (at least 1 with `at1`) zeros and MK with `zi` to nv (at least 1) zeros, calls `sr_views()` and returns 0.

Point helpers. `vx(i)`, `vy(i)` and `vz(i)` return VP at 3 i, 3 i plus 1 and 3 i plus 2. `hx(c)`, `hy(c)` and `hz(c)` return HV at 3 c, 3 c plus 1 and 3 c plus 2.

`setface(t, a, b, c)` stores triangle slot t with corners a, b and c (candidate indices) in TR, sets TA[t] to 1, and computes FN: the normal is the cross product of (corner b less corner a) and (corner c less corner a), divided by its length when the length is above 1e-300 (left as is otherwise), and the offset is the dot product of that normal with corner a. `fdist(t, x, y, z)` returns the dot product of FN's normal for slot t with the point, less FN's offset for slot t.

`orient(t, cx, cy, cz)`: when fdist(t, cx, cy, cz) is above 0.0 (the inside point is in front of the face), it calls setface(t) again with the second and third corners swapped, so the normal points away from the inside point.

The exported function `sr_hull()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, builds the convex hull. Inputs: IN[0] the number of sample directions nd (when not above 3.0 it is 400), IN[1] the relative tolerance (when not above 0.0 it is 1e-9).

1. Candidates. When NV is 0, OUT[0], OUT[1] and OUT[2] become 0.0, 0.0 and 1.0 and it returns 1. Otherwise MK is set to 0 everywhere. The size s is the largest of the extents of the input points along x, y and z (the largest less the smallest coordinate), raised to at least 1e-30, and eps is IN[1] (or its default) times s. For each sample direction i below nd: with y equal to 1.0 minus 2.0 times (i plus 0.5) over nd, r the square root of the larger of 0.0 and 1.0 minus y squared, and the angle i times pi times (3.0 minus the square root of 5.0), the direction is (cos of the angle times r, y, sin of the angle times r); the point with the largest dot product with the direction (the first one on ties) gets MK set to 1. The six points with the smallest and largest x, y and z are marked too. NC becomes the count of marked points; CI, HV and OV are sized for NC (at least 1) with CI holding each marked point's index in input order and HV its coordinates; OV is filled with -1. CAP becomes 2 times NC plus 8, and FN (4 CAP), TR (3 CAP), TA (CAP) and HE (6 CAP) are sized to zeros. NT is 0.
2. Start. p0 is candidate 0. p1 is the candidate farthest from p0. p2 is the candidate farthest from the line through p0 and p1 (the length of the cross product of (p1 less p0) and (point less p0), over the length from p0 to p1). p3 is the candidate farthest from the plane through p0, p1 and p2 (the absolute dot product of the plane's unit normal with the point less p0). When the distance from p0 to p1 is not above eps, OUT[2] becomes 1.0 (a point); when the distance to the line is not above eps, OUT[2] becomes 2.0 (a line); when the distance to the plane is not above eps, OUT[2] becomes 3.0 (flat); in each of those cases OUT[0] becomes NC as real, OUT[1] becomes 0.0 and it returns the flag as int. Otherwise the inside point (cx, cy, cz) is the average of p0, p1, p2 and p3, and the four faces (p0, p1, p2), (p0, p1, p3), (p0, p2, p3) and (p1, p2, p3) go in slots 0 to 3 with setface and are each turned outward with orient; NT is 4.
3. Grow. For each candidate q that is not p0, p1, p2 or p3, in order: it is outside when fdist over some active slot t below NT is above eps. When it is outside: the horizon is found from the directed edges of the active slots that q is in front of (fdist above eps): each such slot's edges (a to b, b to c, c to a) are checked, and an edge from u to w is a horizon edge when the slot on its other side (the active slot holding the directed edge w to u) is not in front of q; every in-front slot is then made inactive (TA 0) after all its edges are checked, and the horizon edges are stored in HE with their count nh. For each horizon edge (u, w) a new triangle (u, w, q) is stored in the first inactive slot below NT, or at NT (raising NT by 1) when none is free, and turned outward with orient. When NT would reach CAP the growth stops and OUT[3] becomes 1.0.
4. Output. The active slots are counted into NT2. OV gives each candidate used by an active slot a new number in candidate order; HV is compacted so that output vertex k sits at 3 k (moving each used candidate down to its new number in candidate order, which never overwrites a candidate not yet moved). OT is sized to 3 NT2 (at least 1) with `zi` and filled with the renumbered corners of the active slots in slot order. OUT[0] becomes the output vertex count as real, OUT[1] NT2 as real and OUT[2] 0.0.

Before every return after step 1 has sized its arrays (the flat cases of step 2 and the end), it calls `sr_views()` so JavaScript sees the resized arrays. Returns 0.

Eigen helper. `jacobi(a00, a01, a02, a11, a12, a22)` finds the eigenvectors of the symmetric 3 by 3 matrix with those entries by cyclic Jacobi rotations (up to 50 sweeps, stopping when the sum of squares of the off-diagonal entries is below 1e-30 times (1.0 plus the sum of squares of the diagonal entries)), and stores the three unit eigenvectors as the columns of the rotation into BX[3] to BX[11]: axis k is BX[3 plus 3 k], BX[4 plus 3 k], BX[5 plus 3 k]. It returns 0.

The exported function `sr_box()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, fits a box around the first OUT[0] as int points of HV (call after sr_hull; when that count is 0 it uses the NV input points VP instead). With m the point count: when m is 0 it fills BX with 0.0 and returns 1. The mean of the points is taken, and their covariance (the sums of the products of the offsets from the mean, over m) goes to `jacobi`. The points are projected on the three axes and the smallest and largest values along each are found; the oriented volume is the product of the three spans. The axis-aligned box of the same points is also measured; when its volume is not above the oriented volume times (1.0 plus 1e-9), the axes become the x, y and z unit vectors and the spans and limits are the axis-aligned ones. BX[12], BX[13] and BX[14] become half of each span; the center is the sum over the three axes of the axis times the middle of its smallest and largest value, stored in BX[0] to BX[2]. OUT[4] becomes the box volume (the product of the three spans). Returns 0.

Mesh helpers. `tri(k, ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz, cx, cy, cz)` stores output triangle k: MP from 9 k holds the three corners in order, and MN from 9 k holds three copies of the unit normal, the cross product of (b less a) and (c less a) divided by its length (left as is when the length is not above 1e-300). `seg(k, ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz)` stores line k in ML from 6 k.

The exported function `sr_mesh(kind, cosang)`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 parameter kind (converted with `as int` at the top), a real parameter cosang and an i32 result, builds a flat-shaded display mesh. Kind 1 is the hull from the last `sr_hull` (OUT[1] as int triangles in OT over the OUT[0] as int points in HV), kind 2 the box in BX from the last `sr_box`.

For the hull with nt triangles: MP and MN are sized to 9 nt (at least 1) zeros with `zf` and triangle k is stored with `tri` from its three corners in HV. Lines: an edge is a pair of triangles sharing two corners; for each triangle i and each of its edges from u to w (u to w, w to x, x to u for corners u, w, x), the other triangle j (j not i) holding the edge from w to u is found, and the edge becomes a line when i is below j and the dot product of the two triangles' normals is below cosang. ML is sized to 6 times 3 nt (at least 1) zeros first, the lines are stored with `seg` and counted into nl.

For the box: with center c, axes e0, e1, e2 and half sizes h0, h1, h2 from BX, corner index b from 0 to 7 is c plus s0 h0 e0 plus s1 h1 e1 plus s2 h2 e2, where s0 is minus 1.0 when b mod 2 is 0 and 1.0 otherwise, s1 the same for (b over 2, int division) mod 2 and s2 for b over 4. MP and MN are sized to 9 times 12 and ML to 6 times 12. The 12 triangles are the corner index triples (0,2,1), (1,2,3), (4,5,6), (5,7,6), (0,1,4), (1,5,4), (2,6,3), (3,6,7), (0,4,2), (2,4,6), (1,3,5), (3,7,5), each stored with `tri` and then, when its normal points toward the center c (the dot product of the normal with the triangle's first corner less c is below 0.0), stored again with its second and third corners swapped. The 12 lines are the corner pairs that differ in exactly one of s0, s1, s2: (0,1), (2,3), (4,5), (6,7), (0,2), (1,3), (4,6), (5,7), (0,4), (1,5), (2,6), (3,7); nl is 12 and nt is 12. When the box's three half sizes are all 0.0 nt and nl are 0.

Afterward OUT[5] becomes nt as real and OUT[7] nl as real, and it calls `sr_views()`. Returns nt as i32.

The `main()` function does nothing.
